{
  "$schema": "https://ui.shadcn.com/schema/registry.json",
  "name": "pmndrs",
  "homepage": "https://github.com/pmndrs/design-system",
  "items": [
    {
      "name": "md3-base",
      "type": "registry:lib",
      "title": "MD3 plumbing",
      "description": "The MD3 colour layer without any colours: the package's Tailwind plugin, its shadcn remap, and the pmndrs seed. Install this only if you compute the palette yourself — otherwise install `theme`, which supplies one.",
      "author": "pmndrs",
      "dependencies": [
        "material-theme-builder@^5.2.0"
      ],
      "files": [
        {
          "path": "registry/md3-base/md3.ts",
          "type": "registry:lib"
        }
      ],
      "css": {
        "@plugin 'material-theme-builder/tailwind'": {},
        "@import 'material-theme-builder/shadcn.css'": {}
      },
      "docs": "**This item ships no colours.** It is the plumbing: two lines of CSS pointing at the package — its Tailwind plugin, and the stylesheet remapping shadcn's variables onto MD3 roles — plus `lib/md3.ts`, the pmndrs seed.\n\nInstall it alone only if you compute the palette yourself. If you just want pmndrs colours, install `theme` instead — it adds a baked palette and the mono font on top of this and needs nothing mounted.\n\nNothing renders until something defines `--md-sys-color-*`, and it does not fail gracefully: the remap here overrides the *stock* shadcn variables, so an undefined palette takes `--background`, `--primary` and `--border` down with it, silently — no error from Tailwind, none from the browser.\n\nEmit it from a React Server Component. `builder` is the root export and carries no `'use client'`, so no palette code reaches the browser:\n\n```tsx\nimport { builder } from 'material-theme-builder'\nimport { pmndrsMtb } from '@/lib/md3'\n\nconst { source, ...rest } = pmndrsMtb\nconst css = builder(source, rest).toCss()\n// <style dangerouslySetInnerHTML={{ __html: css }} /> in <head>\n```\n\nThat repeats the palette in every document — around 32 kB raw, but 2 kB brotli, since it is all hex declarations. Only worth hoisting into your stylesheet with a build step if you have measured that it matters; the build step costs a generated file and an import-order rule that fails silently whenever your seed matches the baked default.\n\nComputed here, the palette is the baked `theme` one, colour for colour: both are `builder(pmndrsMtb)`.\n\n`<Mtb>` from `material-theme-builder/react` does the same as a client component. Avoid it in an app that renders on the server, but it is the right tool where there is no build to hook — a Storybook preview decorator, for instance. With `next-themes`, nest `<ThemeProvider>` inside it, not around it.\n\nThe seed ships seven custom colours, the brand ones: `lime`, `teal`, `cyan`, `purple`, `red`, `orange` and `yellow`, and `theme` bakes their roles. Their utilities are not automatic: the `@plugin` line this item added to your CSS is installed in statement form (`@plugin '...';`), and the registry cannot give it a body, so give it one yourself:\n\n```css\n@plugin \"material-theme-builder/tailwind\" {\n  custom-colors: lime, teal, cyan, purple, red, orange, yellow;\n}\n```\n\nNeed a colour M3 has no role for? Spread `pmndrsMtb` into your own config and add `customColors` there, rather than editing the installed file — `blend: true` harmonizes them against the pmndrs seed, and `...pmndrsMtb.customColors` keeps the seven above. Then add yours to the same body, `custom-colors: lime, teal, cyan, purple, red, orange, yellow, note;`.\n\nFour roles and eleven shades follow per colour — `bg-note`, `text-on-note`, `bg-note-container`, `text-on-note-container`, `bg-note-50` … `bg-note-950`. Nothing else to write."
    },
    {
      "name": "font-mono",
      "type": "registry:font",
      "title": "pmndrs mono font",
      "description": "Inconsolata as `--font-mono`, so `font-mono` resolves to the pmndrs monospace — applied to `code, kbd, samp, pre` only. Through `next/font/google` on Next, through `@fontsource-variable/inconsolata` elsewhere.",
      "author": "pmndrs",
      "font": {
        "family": "Inconsolata",
        "provider": "google",
        "import": "Inconsolata",
        "variable": "--font-mono",
        "dependency": "@fontsource-variable/inconsolata",
        "selector": "code, kbd, samp, pre"
      },
      "docs": "Inconsolata, the pmndrs monospace, as `--font-mono`: `font-mono` resolves to it, and `code`, `kbd`, `samp` and `pre` use it without a class.\n\n- **You usually get this through `theme`**, which depends on it. Install it alone only if you want the font without the palette.\n- **On Next.js** it loads through `next/font/google`. Elsewhere it installs `@fontsource-variable/inconsolata` and imports it.\n- **Scoped to `code, kbd, samp, pre`.** The rest of your UI keeps its sans font.\n- **Named after its role, not the typeface.** If pmndrs changes its mono font, this item changes with it and your install command stays the same."
    },
